            <ArticleTitle>[Prevalence and determinants of obesity in children and young people in Catalonia, Spain, 2006-2012].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>475-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.medcli.2013.10.031</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0025-7753(14)00121-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To estimate the prevalence of obesity and overweight in children aged 2 to 14 years in Catalonia, its trends between 2006 and 2010-2012, and to evaluate social determinants and lifestyle associated with obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A cross-sectional study, using the data from 4,389 surveys from Catalonia Health Surveys of the years 2006 and 2010-2012, was conducted. Obesity and overweight were determined by World Health Organization criteria. Socioeconomic position, parent's education, usual diet and activity, and hours of sleep were assessed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The prevalence of obesity and overweight were: 15.4 and 20.2% in 2010-2012. The prevalence of obesity was higher in boys (15.5%) compared to girls (12.8%) (P&lt;.05), and higher in children ranging from 2 to 9 years old (17.5%) than children aged 10 to 14 years (7.7%) (P&lt;.05). In 2006, the prevalence of obesity and overweight were similar to the prevalence in 2010-2012. Obesity was more frequent in children with parents with a low socioeconomic position and/or a primary or elementary parent's education.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">During the last 5 years (2006-2012), child obesity and overweight have remained stable in Catalonia, yet they are relatively high in Europe. Public health programmes against obesity must consider conducting an intersectional action taking social determinants and family life styles into account.</AbstractText>
